gDd(){return"Clinical algorithm"},
gDc(){return"The clinical management module integrates the type of endometriosis and the predominant symptom to guide management. In patients with pain, initial management may include medical treatment, reserving surgery for refractory cases or significant anatomical involvement. In infertility, medical treatment does not improve pregnancy rates, so surgery or assisted reproductive techniques should be considered according to age, infertility duration, ovarian reserve, tubal patency, and male factor. In asymptomatic patients, expectant management is often appropriate. All decisions must be individualized according to the clinical context."},
gDl(){return"Integrated clinical interpretation"},
gDk(){return"No single classification defines clinical management by itself. rASRM describes classic anatomical extent, #Enzian provides precision in deep disease, and EFI guides postoperative reproductive prognosis. Correct interpretation requires integrating these systems with age, symptoms, history, imaging, surgical findings, and reproductive goals. The application organizes the information, but the final decision always depends on clinical judgment."},
gDi(){return"The #Enzian classification describes endometriosis by anatomical compartments. Compartments A, B, and C correspond to the vagina/rectovaginal septum, uterosacral ligaments/pelvic wall, and rectum/sigmoid. Extension is classified as grades 1, 2, and 3 according to lesion size: 1 (<1 cm), 2 (1\u20133 cm), and 3 (>3 cm). Additional compartments include P (peritoneum), O (ovary), T (tube/tubo-ovarian adhesions), and F locations such as bladder, ureter, and bowel. It is especially useful for surgical planning and communication between specialists."},
gDg(){return"The Endometriosis Fertility Index (EFI) is a postoperative fertility prognostic index. It estimates the probability of spontaneous pregnancy after surgery for endometriosis. It integrates age, infertility duration, reproductive history, surgical findings, and tubo-ovarian function. It should not be used without a complete surgical evaluation, as it loses clinical validity outside that context."},
